### What is the Word Break problem?

Given a string and a dictionary of words, determine whether the string can be segmented into a space-separated sequence of one or more dictionary words. For example, `"leetcode"` with dictionary `["leet", "code"]` is segmentable as `"leet code"`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class WordBreak {
    public static boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> wordDict) {
        Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>(wordDict);
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[n + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (dp[j] && dict.contains(s.substring(j, i))) {
                    dp[i] = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(wordBreak("leetcode", List.of("leet", "code"))); // true
        System.out.println(wordBreak("catsandog", List.of("cats", "dog", "sand", "and", "cat"))); // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### Why does a greedy approach fail?

A greedy approach that always takes the longest (or shortest) matching word can fail. Consider `"catsand"` with dictionary `["cat", "cats", "and", "sand"]`. Greedy longest-first picks `"cats"` leaving `"and"` which works. But change the string to `"catsandog"`: greedy picks `"cats"` leaving `"andog"` where no valid split exists. The DP explores all breakpoints.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class GreedyFails {
    public static boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> dict) {
        Set<String> set = new HashSet<>(dict);
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[s.length() + 1];
        dp[0] = true;
        for (int i = 1; i <= s.length(); i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
                if (dp[j] && set.contains(s.substring(j, i))) { dp[i] = true; break; }
        return dp[s.length()];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Greedy longest-first would pick "cats" + "and" but miss that "cat" + "sand" also works
        System.out.println(wordBreak("catsand", List.of("cat", "cats", "and", "sand"))); // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### How does the DP transition work?

The DP array has size n+1. `dp[0] = true` because the empty prefix is trivially segmentable. For each end position i from 1 to n, try every start position j from 0 to i-1. If `dp[j]` is true and the substring from j to i is a dictionary word, then `dp[i]` is true. The answer is `dp[n]`.

The intuition is that a prefix is segmentable exactly when it ends with a valid word whose starting point is itself a segmentable boundary.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class DPTransitionExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "applepenapple";
        Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>(List.of("apple", "pen"));
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[n + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (dp[j] && dict.contains(s.substring(j, i))) {
                    dp[i] = true;
                    System.out.println("dp[" + i + "]=true via word '" + s.substring(j, i) + "' from j=" + j);
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Result: " + dp[n]);
    }
}
```

```text
dp[5]=true via word 'apple' from j=0
dp[8]=true via word 'pen' from j=5
dp[13]=true via word 'apple' from j=8
Result: true
```

### How can you optimise with max word length?

Instead of trying all j from 0 to i-1, limit j to `i - maxWordLength` since no dictionary word is longer than `maxWordLength`. This reduces the inner loop from O(n) to O(maxWordLength).

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class WordBreakOptimised {
    public static boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> wordDict) {
        Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>(wordDict);
        int maxLen = wordDict.stream().mapToInt(String::length).max().orElse(0);
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[n + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = Math.max(0, i - maxLen); j < i; j++) {
                if (dp[j] && dict.contains(s.substring(j, i))) {
                    dp[i] = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(wordBreak("applepenapple", List.of("apple", "pen"))); // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings, single-character dictionaries, strings that cannot be split at all, and strings where every character is a dictionary word.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class WordBreakEdgeCases {
    public static boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> dict) {
        Set<String> set = new HashSet<>(dict);
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[s.length() + 1];
        dp[0] = true;
        for (int i = 1; i <= s.length(); i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
                if (dp[j] && set.contains(s.substring(j, i))) { dp[i] = true; break; }
        return dp[s.length()];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + wordBreak("", List.of("a")));                    // true
        System.out.println("No split: " + wordBreak("xyz", List.of("a", "b")));        // false
        System.out.println("Each char: " + wordBreak("abc", List.of("a", "b", "c"))); // true
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
No split: false
Each char: true
```

### What about Word Break II (return all sentences)?

The follow-up Word Break II asks you to return all possible segmentations, not just whether one exists. The output can be exponentially large, so use memoised backtracking rather than simple DP.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class WordBreakII {
    public static List<String> wordBreak(String s, List<String> wordDict) {
        Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>(wordDict);
        Map<Integer, List<String>> memo = new HashMap<>();
        return backtrack(s, 0, dict, memo);
    }

    private static List<String> backtrack(String s, int start, Set<String> dict, Map<Integer, List<String>> memo) {
        if (memo.containsKey(start)) return memo.get(start);
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (start == s.length()) { result.add(""); return result; }
        for (int end = start + 1; end <= s.length(); end++) {
            String word = s.substring(start, end);
            if (dict.contains(word)) {
                for (String sub : backtrack(s, end, dict, memo)) {
                    result.add(word + (sub.isEmpty() ? "" : " " + sub));
                }
            }
        }
        memo.put(start, result);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(wordBreak("catsanddog", List.of("cat", "cats", "and", "sand", "dog")));
    }
}
```

```text
[cat sand dog, cats and dog]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The basic Word Break DP runs in O(n^2) time for the two nested loops, with each iteration costing up to O(L) for substring creation and HashSet lookup, giving O(n^2 * L) total. Space is O(n) for the DP array. The max-word-length optimisation reduces the inner loop to O(maxLen) iterations.

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, a legacy channel sends payment instructions as one concatenated string of command words with no separators. Rajesh's parser uses this DP to verify a clean split before the execution engine processes the commands. A greedy longest-word match could fail because an early wrong cut blocks a valid later split, so Rajesh uses the DP that remembers every reachable boundary.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class PaymentInstructionParser {
    private final Set<String> commands;

    public PaymentInstructionParser(List<String> validCommands) {
        this.commands = new HashSet<>(validCommands);
    }

    public boolean canParse(String instruction) {
        int n = instruction.length();
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[n + 1];
        dp[0] = true;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
                if (dp[j] && commands.contains(instruction.substring(j, i))) { dp[i] = true; break; }
        return dp[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PaymentInstructionParser parser = new PaymentInstructionParser(
            List.of("transfer", "deposit", "balance", "check")
        );
        System.out.println(parser.canParse("transferbalancecheck")); // true
        System.out.println(parser.canParse("transferbal"));          // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```
